 Let R1, R2, and R3 be the set of all the Republicans in the Senate committee (n(R) = 5). H is the set of randomly chosen subcommittee of 3 members. Therefore the size of the set H is n(H). The set H can be partitioned into three disjoint sets: H1 = {three Republicans selected}, H2 = {two Republicans selected and one Democrat selected}, and H3 = {two Republicans selected and one Independent selected}. Thus we can say the following: <br/> $n(H)=n(H1)+n(H2)+n(H3)$ <br/> The size of the set H1 is $n(H1)=n(R)C(3)$, where $n(R)$ as mentioned above is the size of the set of randomly chosen subcommittee and $C(3)$ is the number of combinations of 3 members that can be selected from 5 members. Therefore, $n(H1)=5C(3)$ <br/> By symmetry, $n(H2)=6C(2)C(1)$ and $n(H3)=2C(1)C(1)$. Therefore, $n(H)=5C(3)+6C(2)C(1)+2C(1)C(1)$. Each of these three sets is equally probable, therefore the probability of obtaining a subcommittee of only Republicans is <br/> $P(R-R-R)=\frac{n(H1)}{n(H)}=\frac{5C(3)}{5C(3)+6C(2)+2C(1)}$ <br/> <br/> Now if we redo our calculation by only considering the subcommittee of 3 members that are randomly selected, we have: <br/> Let Y be the subcommittee of 3 members that are randomly selected from the panel of senators. The size of the set Y is $n(Y)=13C(3)$ where 13 is the total number of senators and 3 is the size of a randomly selected subcommittee. We consider two events: <br/> "R1 is inclusion in a randomly selected subcommittee", i.e., $E_1$ <br/> "R2 is inclusion in the randomly selected subcommittee", i.e., $E_2$ <br/> "R3 is inclusion in a randomly selected subcommittee", i.e., $E_3$ <br/> Therefore: <br/> $E_1,E_2,E_3$ form a covering and are independent. <br/> $P(E_1)=P(R_1)\cdot P(R_2|R_1)\cdot P(R_3|R_1,R_2)=(5/13)\cdot(4/12)\cdot(3/11)\;$ and symmetrical $P(E_2)=P(R_2)\cdot P(R_1|R_2)\cdot P(R_3|R_1,R_2)=(6/13)\cdot(5/12)\cdot(4/11)\;$ and $P(E_3)=2/13\cdot 1/12 \cdot 1/11$. We can say that the sequence R1,R2,R3 occur with probability $P(E_1)+P(E_2)+P(E_3)$, and thus the interviewer can estimate the probability of observing a subcommittee of only Republicans to be: <br/> $P(E)=P(E_1)\cdot P(E_2|E_1)+P(E_2)\cdot P(E_3|E_2)+P(E_3)\cdot P(R_3|R_1,R_2,R_3)=0.232+0.119+0.004=0.355$. <br/> The probability that all 3 members are Republicans is $P(R-R-R)=P(E_1)\cdot P(E_2|E_1)\cdot P(E_3|E_1,E_2)=P(E_1)\cdot P(E_3|E_1)=0.232$.
